body:
**Shape.** `var y := new R(new Q()).f; y.Use();` — a borrow of an unbound owned temporary is bound to a local, and the temporary is neither disposed at the end of its full expression nor ever.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body()
    var y := new R(2, new Q(1)).f;   // a borrow of an unbound temporary escapes its full expression (K8.4)
    y.Use();                          // the temporary was never disposed either (K8.1)
  end Body;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(): int
    R.Body();
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35, both modes:** compiles with zero diagnostics; output `U:1` then `END`, exit 0. **No `D:` line at all**: the temporary `R#2`/`Q#1` is leaked (the spec requires `D:2 D:1` right after the first statement, and then the `y.Use()` would fault in checked mode — `DisposedAccessException`, exit 70 — because `y` borrows a disposed object). Both halves are visible on one two-line program.

**Spec (v0.345).**
- K8.4 (§28.3.1): *"A temporary is an admissible borrow source only within its boundary; a borrow or frame that would escape it is **rejected, never lifetime-extended** … The escapes rejected are every escape K2.3 can compare — among them a binding, store, capture, `return` or `yield return` of the borrow in the producing body"*. `var y := new R(new Q()).f;` is exactly the binding escape. Expected: a compile error at the declaration.
- K8.1 / §28.3.11: *"Unbound owned temporaries are disposed at their full expression's end, after their uses, in reverse production order"* — *"the result of an `owned`-returning call (or other owned producer) that is not bound to any name … used only by borrowing operations within the expression — as the receiver of a further call, as a non-`owned` argument, as an operator operand, **as a member or index access** … is disposed at the end of the full expression"*. Expected: `D:2`, `D:1` after the first statement even if the escape were admitted.

**Related.** #6775 reports the non-disposal half for a temporary used as a *call receiver* or argument; this shape is a *field read* and adds the escape half (the K8.4 rejection is missing, so the leak is observable as a silently-accepted dangling alias once the disposal lands: fixing #6775 alone turns this program into a checked-mode fault at `y.Use()` instead of a rejection).

**Differential numbers.** Every program of the model's `temps` fragment containing this statement form (`tbrw`) is a level-1 disagreement: 716 of 3,000 sampled programs at vars 3 / len 4 are accepted by r35 and rejected by the model under K8.4; the remainder with `tbrw` are rejected by r35 for a *later* statement only.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
